Here's an example of a parent component that fetches data from an API and passes it to a child component:

**Parent Component (Parent.vue)**
```vue
<script setup>
import { ref, onMounted } from 'vue'
import ChildComponent from './ChildComponent.vue'

const data = ref(null)
const loading = ref(true)
const error = ref(null)

onMounted(async () => {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('/api/data')
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error('Failed to fetch data')
    }
    data.value = await response.json()
  } catch (err) {
    error.value = err.message
  } finally {
    loading.value = false
  }
})
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    <div v-if="loading">Loading...</div>
    <div v-else-if="error">Error: {{ error }}</div>
    <ChildComponent v-else :data="data" />
  </div>
</template>
```

**Child Component (ChildComponent.vue)**
```vue
<script setup>
defineProps({
  data: {
    type: Object,
    required: true
  }
})
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    <h1>{{ data.title }}</h1>
    <p>{{ data.description }}</p>
  </div>
</template>
```

**Key points:**

- The parent fetches data in `onMounted` and stores it in a `ref`
- The child component is only rendered when data is available (`v-else`)
- Props are passed using `:data="data"` (the `v-bind` shorthand)
- The child declares the prop with `defineProps`
- The `loading` and `error` states handle the async flow gracefully

